WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.000
So today I want to explain how we can actually estimate the intrinsic as well as extrinsic parameters of our camera, which is called the so-called direct linear transform.

00:11.000 --> 00:29.000
So what we want to do is we want to estimate the five intrinsic parameters, so the camera constant, the offset in x and y of the principal point, potential shear, as well as the scale difference of those five intrinsic parameters which are elements of the calibration matrix.

00:29.000 --> 00:37.000
We want to estimate that, as well as the location of the camera, under the assumption that we know a certain number of fixed points in the environment.

00:37.000 --> 00:46.000
So assume we have the 3D coordinates of a set of points in the scene, can we actually estimate the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of our camera?

00:46.000 --> 00:50.000
And yes, we can do this, and that's what I want to talk about today.

00:50.000 --> 01:00.000
So if you want to describe how a point from the 3D world is actually mapped into a pixel coordinate, we have this equation x equals px.

01:00.000 --> 01:15.000
And we discussed intensively in the lecture on the intrinsic and extrinsic, how those parameters are sitting inside p, what do they mean, and how they impact the process of mapping a 3D point from the world into our image.

01:15.000 --> 01:28.000
What we want to do today is to actually estimate those parameters, so to find values for those parameters, given that we know some of the points in the world, and given that we know where they are mapped to in our image.

01:28.000 --> 01:34.000
So today, these x's are given, and we want to compute the parameters of p.

01:34.000 --> 01:45.000
So assume we know a certain number of so-called control points here, indicated by those red crosses distributed over our scene, we want to estimate the intrinsic and extrinsic of our camera.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
So that's the task we are heading for today.

01:48.000 --> 02:03.000
So one wanted our intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of our camera, what is given are the coordinates of the object in the 3D world, as well as observations of the 3D locations of those points in our image.

02:03.000 --> 02:10.000
And we assume to know the data association, so we know which point is mapped to which pixel in our image.

02:10.000 --> 02:21.000
And based on this information, so based on the known 3D coordinates of the control points, and based on the observations, we want to estimate the parameters in p, so the intrinsic and the extrinsic.

02:21.000 --> 02:29.000
And the mapping works using this DLT, that's something that we have described, so we have our camera illustrated here with our projection center.

02:29.000 --> 02:35.000
So this is the origin of the coordinate system of our camera.

02:35.000 --> 02:44.000
We have our image plane shown over here, and then the point in 3D on an object is projected onto our image plane.

02:44.000 --> 02:47.000
And this was kind of this model that we are using.

02:47.000 --> 02:55.000
In reality, of course, the image plane sits behind here in that camera, but we can rotate it to the front and then have a coordinate system over here.

02:55.000 --> 03:03.000
So we know the small x, we know the large x, and we want to know the parameters of the overall mapping process.

03:03.000 --> 03:20.000
So what was in there in this process are the extrinsic parameters, which is the location of the camera as well as its orientation, this is x0 and r, and the intrinsic, the calibration parameters, which are stored here in k.

03:20.000 --> 03:23.000
And these are the elements that we want to estimate.

03:23.000 --> 03:30.000
So we can actually write that down and also see this is a 3x1 vector, so two dimensions in homogeneous coordinates.

03:31.000 --> 03:44.000
There's a 3x3 calibration matrix, a 3x3 rotation matrix, there's a 3x1 vector for the shift, and the 4x1 point of the point in the 3D world expressed in homogeneous coordinates.

03:44.000 --> 03:54.000
And all the parameters of p are stored here in this part, which overall sums up to a 3x4 matrix.

03:54.000 --> 04:05.000
So we have the 3x4 matrix, the camera internal parameters, the so-called intrinsics, are stored inside k, and the extrinsics are the postparameters of the camera.

04:05.000 --> 04:19.000
That means the translation, three dimensions, and the rotations, three dimensions, given through this x0 and the rotation matrix, and together this is this DLT transformation we have been talking about.

04:20.000 --> 04:34.000
So what we want to do today, given this, and given this, we want to estimate three translation parameters, three rotation parameters, and the five parameters of my calibration matrix.

04:34.000 --> 04:36.000
So that's the task of today.

04:37.000 --> 04:44.000
The first question that we should ask ourselves, how many points do we actually need to know in the environment and observe in our image?

04:44.000 --> 04:51.000
So we have this equation x equals px, so how many of these x do I actually need in order to solve my problem?

04:51.000 --> 04:59.000
In order to do that, let's look at how many unknowns we have and how many information one of those observations actually generates.

04:59.000 --> 05:10.000
So if we say this is a point in 2D and this is a point in 3D, but expressed in homogeneous coordinates, this would be a 3D vector here and a 4D vector over here.

05:10.000 --> 05:15.000
However, we are not interested in the points in homogeneous coordinates. In the end, we want to obtain Euclidean coordinates.

05:15.000 --> 05:21.000
That means we need to normalize them away, basically dividing by the last component.

05:21.000 --> 05:31.000
So we divide this vector by the last component and this vector by the last component, which gives us the Euclidean coordinates over here and the Euclidean coordinates over there.

05:31.000 --> 05:37.000
And then we basically need to take our projection matrix p and multiply it with those elements.

05:37.000 --> 05:50.000
So what we in the end obtain is this form here and if you write them down, basically expand all the multiplications here, then we obtain two equations for a single observation.

05:50.000 --> 05:56.000
So if you see one point, we get two observation equations, one for the x coordinate and one for the y coordinate.

05:57.000 --> 06:13.000
And at the same point in time here, we have these two equations and we have the unknowns or the unknowns that we need to estimate are all the parameters which are sitting in here.

06:13.000 --> 06:19.000
And remember, the DLT has 11 degrees of freedom, 5 plus 6.

06:19.000 --> 06:34.000
So if we have 11 unknowns in here and we get two observations for every point that we see, that means we need 11 divided by 2, which would be 5.5.

06:34.000 --> 06:40.000
So we need six points in the environment in order to being able to estimate that p here.

06:40.000 --> 06:45.000
So at least six control points we need to know in order to estimate all our parameters.

06:45.000 --> 06:50.000
And this is different from what is called spatial resectioning, for example, versus DLT.

06:50.000 --> 06:54.000
So spatial resectioning basically localizes already calibrated camera.

06:54.000 --> 06:59.000
Then I have only six unknowns and need to have at least three points in the environment.

06:59.000 --> 07:03.000
But we are looking here today is actually more interested in the camera calibration part.

07:03.000 --> 07:08.000
So determining the intrinsic parameters, but also the location of our camera, then we have 11 unknowns.

07:08.000 --> 07:15.000
That means we need to have at least six points and this can be solved by this DLT computation.

07:15.000 --> 07:25.000
Of course, under the assumption that we only have an affine camera, that means we are ignoring our nonlinear effects or nonlinear noise that we have in our process.

07:25.000 --> 07:29.000
So we assume only our linear dependencies that we have.

07:30.000 --> 07:35.000
So in sum, the task today is compute the mapping,

07:35.000 --> 07:46.000
determine the parameters and we use the direct linear transform for computing the orientation of an uncalibrated camera using at least six known 3D points in the environment.

07:46.000 --> 07:48.000
So what are we doing?

07:48.000 --> 07:51.000
We are estimating the 11 elements of p.

07:51.000 --> 07:54.000
Remember p has 11 elements.

07:54.000 --> 08:06.000
Although it has 12 numbers, there are also 11 degrees of freedom in there because one is due to the property of the homogeneous matrix that it's only defined up to a scalar.

08:06.000 --> 08:12.000
And what we have given are the 3D coordinates of the points xi and we have at least six of them.

08:12.000 --> 08:17.000
Let's say capital I and we have the observed image coordinates of that point.

08:17.000 --> 08:22.000
So we know which point in the world is mapped into which image coordinate so the data association is given.

08:22.000 --> 08:27.000
And we assume that this function expresses everything we want to express.

08:27.000 --> 08:30.000
So let's see how that works.

08:30.000 --> 08:39.000
So we write down x equals px and expand our matrix px so that we have the 12 elements in there from p11 to p34.

08:39.000 --> 08:42.000
And these are our unknown parameters that we want to estimate.

08:42.000 --> 08:46.000
This x is given and this x over here is given.

08:46.000 --> 08:55.000
The first thing we do, we just slightly rearrange or rewrite that equation by saying, okay, let's take those numbers and group them together as a vector called A.

08:55.000 --> 09:02.000
Let's take those and group them as a vector called B and this one as a vector called C.

09:02.000 --> 09:10.000
So we define three new vectors A, B and C as follows so that A transpose gives me the first row of the matrix P,

09:10.000 --> 09:19.000
B transpose gives me the second row of the matrix P and C transpose gives me the third row of my projection matrix.

09:19.000 --> 09:28.000
So that I then can write the same equation x as A transpose, B transpose, C transpose multiplied with my vector C.

09:28.000 --> 09:34.000
So nothing very bad has happened. We just defined three new variables, three new vectors to regroup them.

09:34.000 --> 09:45.000
If you now multiply these elements with x, it means A gets multiplied with x, B gets multiplied with x and C gets multiplied with x.

09:45.000 --> 09:50.000
And this gives us the one, two, three dimensions of my output.

09:50.000 --> 10:01.000
So my output u, v, w in homogeneous coordinates can be written as A transpose xi, B transpose xi and C transpose xi.

10:01.000 --> 10:10.000
So in order to obtain u, v and w, we only need to compute the product of these two vectors over here.

10:10.000 --> 10:22.000
And again, remember we wanted to estimate that in the end in Euclidean coordinates because we want to have in the end have only this information about the two pixel coordinates that we're getting.

10:22.000 --> 10:27.000
So to turn this into a Euclidean vector, we need to divide those two elements by wi.

10:27.000 --> 10:38.000
So that means it would be a division of A transpose x by C transpose x for the first component and B transpose x divided by C transpose x for the second component.

10:38.000 --> 10:41.000
And we can just kind of write that down.

10:41.000 --> 10:44.000
So our xi in Euclidean coordinates is written over here.

10:44.000 --> 10:54.000
And so in order to perform the normalization to go from the homogeneous world to the Euclidean world, we need to divide by the last component, in this case, C transpose xi.

10:54.000 --> 11:04.000
So that my x coordinate is A transpose xi divided by C transpose xi and my y coordinate is B transpose xi by C transpose xi.

11:05.000 --> 11:15.000
So what we now can do is we can use this equation and this equation to define a system of equations.

11:15.000 --> 11:20.000
So this can be just by multiplying with C transpose xi.

11:20.000 --> 11:27.000
We can bring that to the other side, subtract A transpose x and we get this equation which equals to zero.

11:27.000 --> 11:37.000
We can do exactly the same thing for our second equation for the y coordinate so that from the y coordinate we derive this equation.

11:37.000 --> 11:44.000
And putting those things together, we actually get a system of our linear equations.

11:45.000 --> 11:55.000
So that we can actually rewrite this in a form that here we have minus x transpose A plus x transpose C equals to zero.

11:55.000 --> 12:01.000
And here x transpose B y times x transpose C equals to zero.

12:01.000 --> 12:04.000
Why are we doing this? Why are we writing this in this way?

12:04.000 --> 12:12.000
Because here in this setup, the A, B, C are in the end our unknowns, our parameters we want to estimate.

12:12.000 --> 12:17.000
And the capital X and lowercase x and y are our known parameters.

12:17.000 --> 12:23.000
So it's kind of slightly differently how you would rewrite a system of linear equations where x is typically your unknown.

12:23.000 --> 12:30.000
Here the x are our unknowns and A, B, C are kind of the elements that we in the end want to estimate.

12:30.000 --> 12:35.000
Therefore we write it in this form that our unknowns do not have the transposition over here.

12:36.000 --> 12:45.000
Okay, so that means we can actually collect a parameter vector P where we stick A, B, C together.

12:45.000 --> 12:49.000
And this can be kind of a vectorization of my matrix P transpose.

12:49.000 --> 12:53.000
It's just kind of sticking those 12 elements on top of each other.

12:53.000 --> 13:01.000
So this here is nothing else than a 12 by 1 vector containing the individual elements of my matrix P.

13:01.000 --> 13:15.000
And if I use this, I can then rewrite that system of linear equations that I had before into a coefficient vector A transposed for the x coordinates

13:15.000 --> 13:20.000
and A transposed for the y coordinates multiplied with my unknown parameters P.

13:20.000 --> 13:27.000
And so this equation results me in this part and this equation results me in this part over here

13:27.000 --> 13:38.000
because this is the other coefficients which depend on the xi and here we have the coefficients which depend on the ay on the y component over here.

13:38.000 --> 13:46.000
And so if you write this down like this, then P is my vectorized version of P.

13:46.000 --> 13:55.000
My A transposed x is simply minus xi transposed, zero transposed, small xi times capital Xi transposed.

13:55.000 --> 14:00.000
And which we can expand into a vector of this form.

14:00.000 --> 14:07.000
So we have the minus x, y, z coordinate over here of the point in 3D, a minus one and then a couple of zeros.

14:07.000 --> 14:14.000
And then the x coordinate of the image multiplied with the x coordinate, the x coordinate and the image multiplied with the y coordinate of the point,

14:14.000 --> 14:18.000
the x coordinate multiplied with the z coordinate of the point and the xi coordinate.

14:18.000 --> 14:21.000
And we can do exactly the same for y as well.

14:21.000 --> 14:31.000
The only difference is that the zeros are basically sitting here at a different position and those elements are shifted over here, but it's analogous to that.

14:31.000 --> 14:40.000
So let's verify that rewriting that I have done, which we'll use in a few minutes to solve that system, that this is actually still correct.

14:40.000 --> 14:45.000
So that what I've written down here is exactly the same than our original system.

14:45.000 --> 14:48.000
Let's make a quick check and see that we have done everything right.

14:48.000 --> 14:59.000
So we can verify the coordinates by simply computing this product of this coefficient vector and our unknown, which I have written down, just copy paste from our previous slide.

14:59.000 --> 15:05.000
So just as a reminder, this was my vector A, this was my vector B and this down here was my vector C.

15:05.000 --> 15:09.000
So this I can rewrite this as ABC.

15:10.000 --> 15:17.000
And over here, I can take the first four elements and they give me the minus xi.

15:17.000 --> 15:31.000
This is the negative 3D coordinate of the point expressed in homogeneous coordinates, a zero vector and the pixel coordinate multiplied with my 3D location.

15:31.000 --> 15:44.000
And so this is the nothing else than this element multiplied with A, the zero multiplied with B, this goes away and this third element multiplied with C.

15:44.000 --> 15:48.000
So exactly get my equation down here.

15:48.000 --> 15:52.000
So I have nothing else than what I exactly had written down before.

15:52.000 --> 15:55.000
We can do exactly the same thing with B as well.

15:55.000 --> 15:58.000
It's completely identical except that the zero sits over here.

15:58.000 --> 16:02.000
So only the B parts survive and the A part goes to zero.

16:02.000 --> 16:15.000
So by this quick check that A transpose P gives me my original equations, I can now use this form of writing it down as A transpose B in order to do my computation.

16:15.000 --> 16:26.000
So what I need to solve is this thing, I can stack that together as a big coefficient vector, just stacking these A's together, always AX, AY, AX, AY and so on.

16:26.000 --> 16:30.000
So this is a large matrix and my unknown vector P.

16:30.000 --> 16:39.000
And so I can say this is just a regular matrix, let's call that M and the vector P is still my unknowns and this has to be equal to zero.

16:40.000 --> 16:50.000
So what I basically did for all of those observations, so for every observed point, I generate one of those pairs.

16:50.000 --> 17:03.000
So this AXI is for the coefficient about the X coordinates of the point I and the coefficients of the Y coordinate of point I gives me, every point gives me two of those equations.

17:03.000 --> 17:04.000
So I can stack them.

17:04.000 --> 17:10.000
So this thing here in the end is a matrix which is in dimension 2I times 12.

17:10.000 --> 17:18.000
2I because I have two points, I have a number of points and 12 because everything has in here those 12 elements.

17:18.000 --> 17:26.000
So I have this matrix 2I times 12 and this 12 dimensional vector P and this should be equal to zero.

17:26.000 --> 17:39.000
So if I solve this system, then I actually have a solution for my parameters P which then directly define my projection matrix.

17:39.000 --> 17:43.000
So how do we actually solve this MP equals to zero?

17:43.000 --> 17:48.000
This is a special type of linear system, it's a so-called homogeneous system.

17:48.000 --> 17:51.000
That means a system where the right hand side equals zero.

17:51.000 --> 17:56.000
So this is AX equals to not B but zero over here.

17:56.000 --> 18:02.000
And solving this system is equivalent of finding the null space of this matrix A.

18:02.000 --> 18:08.000
So the null space of a matrix A is a vector to which I can multiply A and it will return me zero.

18:08.000 --> 18:13.000
So X must be a vector in the null space of A.

18:13.000 --> 18:14.000
And how to find that null space?

18:14.000 --> 18:23.000
For that we can use the singular value decomposition which is a technique for finding the singular values and singular vectors.

18:23.000 --> 18:31.000
And if I have a singular value that is zero, I know that the corresponding singular vector is part of the null space of A.

18:31.000 --> 18:32.000
So what do we need to do for this?

18:32.000 --> 18:43.000
We need to apply the SVD to our matrix M in order to pick out then the singular vector which corresponds to the singular value of zero.

18:43.000 --> 18:46.000
And that is then a solution to P.

18:46.000 --> 18:50.000
In practice, however, we have the issue that we have redundant observations.

18:50.000 --> 18:55.000
That means we typically have more observations, more equations than we have unknowns.

18:55.000 --> 18:58.000
So M times P will not be exactly zero.

18:59.000 --> 19:05.000
We'll take values close to zero but for a solution but unlikely not exactly zero.

19:05.000 --> 19:10.000
So what we have, we don't have M P equals zero, we have M P equals W with a small W.

19:10.000 --> 19:17.000
And so what we then want to do is we typically want to find a P that minimizes these Ws.

19:17.000 --> 19:20.000
Or what we are doing which minimizes W transpose W.

19:20.000 --> 19:23.000
So kind of the squared of that.

19:23.000 --> 19:29.000
So we have basically our residuals, our error that we want to minimize, W transpose W.

19:29.000 --> 19:33.000
And we want to find the P that minimizes this expression.

19:33.000 --> 19:39.000
So what we do is we just put those elements in here and then we have this expression over here.

19:39.000 --> 19:48.000
So we want to basically minimize the squared error here under the assumption that P is of norm one so that P itself doesn't go to zero.

19:48.000 --> 19:51.000
And in the end the P forms homogeneous matrix.

19:51.000 --> 19:56.000
This homogeneous matrix is anyway defined only up to a scaling constant.

19:56.000 --> 19:59.000
So I don't care about the actual lengths of my vector P.

19:59.000 --> 20:01.000
So how does it work?

20:01.000 --> 20:08.000
We are applying the SVD to the matrix M and the SVD decomposes the matrix M into three matrices.

20:08.000 --> 20:13.000
The matrix U, the matrix S and the matrix V or V transpose depending on your implementation.

20:13.000 --> 20:16.000
And these matrices have different properties.

20:16.000 --> 20:23.000
So this matrix S is a diagonal matrix for example which contains the singular values in descending order.

20:23.000 --> 20:26.000
So the largest first, the smallest one and the last.

20:26.000 --> 20:31.000
So the smallest singular value that we have we find in S1212.

20:31.000 --> 20:36.000
If it's zero it's perfect but typically it is not zero, it's smaller than zero.

20:36.000 --> 20:39.000
It's close to zero but not exactly zero.

20:39.000 --> 20:47.000
The matrix V or V transpose stores the corresponding singular vectors.

20:47.000 --> 20:53.000
So what we can do is we can pick out the singular vector that corresponds to the smallest singular value

20:53.000 --> 21:00.000
and use this as a solution for my P because it will minimize that squared error.

21:00.000 --> 21:07.000
So what I just do is just take this matrix V and take out of this matrix V

21:07.000 --> 21:14.000
the eigensingular vector that corresponds to the smallest singular value

21:14.000 --> 21:16.000
and this is the one which minimizes P.

21:16.000 --> 21:23.000
And this gives me my 12 values of P which I can then rearrange and have my projection matrix.

21:23.000 --> 21:28.000
And this is a straightforward application of the singular value decomposition

21:28.000 --> 21:33.000
that I use for finding such homogeneous systems or finding the null spaces of the matrix

21:33.000 --> 21:38.000
just by determining their singular values and singular vectors.

21:40.000 --> 21:43.000
Okay, so the question is does this always work?

21:43.000 --> 21:47.000
So it actually turns out that it's not always the case.

21:47.000 --> 21:53.000
So let's see, when does M actually has a rank of 11?

21:53.000 --> 21:58.000
And this is the case if I have at least six points or if I have less than six points

21:58.000 --> 22:03.000
that I mentioned, the number of rows that I have will be smaller.

22:03.000 --> 22:09.000
So if I have only five points, I would have ten rows so I can't have a rank of 11.

22:09.000 --> 22:14.000
And under the assumption that we have no gross errors, we need to have at least six points.

22:14.000 --> 22:22.000
The problem is we, however, will get a rank deficiency if all points in the 3D world lie on a single plane.

22:22.000 --> 22:27.000
And let me illustrate how that works because we can see directly from this matrix M.

22:27.000 --> 22:33.000
So again, my matrix M consists of all those blocks for the individual measurements,

22:33.000 --> 22:36.000
so two rows for every observation.

22:36.000 --> 22:41.000
Now assume all points in the 3D world lie on a plane and let's select a certain plane

22:41.000 --> 22:45.000
just to make it easy to visualize here, Z equals to zero.

22:45.000 --> 22:52.000
That means all points in the 3D world have a Z coordinate in the world reference frame that is actually zero.

22:53.000 --> 22:56.000
So what's the effect of that?

22:56.000 --> 23:03.000
The effect of this is that these elements, the whole column will be zero, this column will be zero,

23:03.000 --> 23:05.000
and this column over here will be zero.

23:05.000 --> 23:08.000
So these will lead to zero columns.

23:08.000 --> 23:11.000
So out of these 12 columns that I have, three will be zero.

23:11.000 --> 23:16.000
That means only nine columns will remain, which potentially are not zero everywhere.

23:16.000 --> 23:23.000
So that means the rank is reduced to at maximum nine in that setup.

23:24.000 --> 23:31.000
And so this will lead to a rank deficiency and I will not find an appropriate solution for my parameters.

23:31.000 --> 23:37.000
There is also, so the points lying on a plane, there's also some other critical configurations,

23:37.000 --> 23:43.000
but the, in practice, relevant critical surfaces are actually the points lying on a plane

23:43.000 --> 23:46.000
because that happens actually quite frequently.

23:46.000 --> 23:51.000
If you think that you have a wall where a lot of feature points are located and you know those points,

23:51.000 --> 23:53.000
then all those points lie on a plane.

23:53.000 --> 23:58.000
Or if you are in an airplane mode and you're watching down with an aerial camera

23:58.000 --> 24:02.000
and you have a roughly planar ground and you know a few control points on the ground,

24:02.000 --> 24:06.000
they all lie on a plane or at least approximatively on a plane,

24:06.000 --> 24:10.000
which will lead to the degenerated solution of your projection matrix

24:10.000 --> 24:15.000
and thus you will not be able to estimate your intrinsic and extrinsic parameters.

24:15.000 --> 24:17.000
So from a practical application point of view,

24:17.000 --> 24:22.000
you really need to make sure that your 3D points do not lie on a plane.

24:22.000 --> 24:27.000
They actually have a configuration so that they sufficiently vary from the planar configuration.

24:27.000 --> 24:32.000
Otherwise, you will not get a good solution for your matrix P.

24:32.000 --> 24:35.000
But again, one thing is what happens if we have our matrix P?

24:35.000 --> 24:40.000
If we have P, we still don't know the calibration matrix, the rotation matrix,

24:40.000 --> 24:44.000
or the translation vector where the camera is located in space.

24:44.000 --> 24:48.000
So how I can actually turn P into K, R, and X0?

24:48.000 --> 24:53.000
I mean, the other way is easy going from those parameters to the projection matrix.

24:53.000 --> 24:55.000
Of course, straightforward, that's the way P is defined.

24:55.000 --> 24:57.000
But what about the other way?

24:57.000 --> 25:00.000
How to go from P to those three parameters?

25:00.000 --> 25:03.000
And this is kind of the first time now that this becomes relevant

25:03.000 --> 25:10.000
and there's actually a way for doing this, for turning P into those three matrices.

25:10.000 --> 25:13.000
And for that, let's look to the structure of P.

25:13.000 --> 25:22.000
So the structure, the P is a 3x4 matrix, so it has a 3x3 matrix over here and a vector over here.

25:22.000 --> 25:27.000
So we can simply, as a definition, define a matrix H, which is a 3x3 matrix

25:27.000 --> 25:34.000
given by the first 3x3 block of P, and then a vector H, which is the last column of P.

25:34.000 --> 25:40.000
So H, capital H and small h, the matrix H and the vector H are directly there.

25:40.000 --> 25:43.000
And given that I know what kind of structure P has,

25:43.000 --> 25:50.000
I know that H is the calibration matrix times the rotation matrix, and H has this form over here.

25:50.000 --> 25:55.000
So what can we do in order now to extract K, R, and X0?

25:55.000 --> 26:01.000
The first thing is relatively straightforward, estimating X0.

26:01.000 --> 26:08.000
We can see this if we take this matrix H and invert it, we get Kr in bracket inverse.

26:08.000 --> 26:13.000
And if you multiply it from the left-hand side over here, we're actually eliminating this K and this R,

26:13.000 --> 26:16.000
and this allows us to then obtain X0.

26:16.000 --> 26:21.000
So for getting the projection center that is straightforward,

26:21.000 --> 26:26.000
we just invert the 3x3 block, multiply it from left-hand side to H, change the sign,

26:26.000 --> 26:31.000
and then we get the projection center of our camera.

26:31.000 --> 26:34.000
So that was very easy, very straightforward.

26:35.000 --> 26:40.000
The next thing, getting the rotation matrix out and the calibration matrix is more tricky.

26:40.000 --> 26:46.000
So what we now need to do is we have this matrix H, which is K times R.

26:46.000 --> 26:51.000
And so how can we actually break this single matrix into two matrices

26:51.000 --> 26:57.000
so that they both have the properties of the one is the calibration matrix and the other one is the rotation matrix?

26:57.000 --> 27:02.000
So in order to do this, we need to ask ourselves, what do we know actually about this matrix K and this matrix R?

27:03.000 --> 27:12.000
Can we exploit some of the properties in there so that we can appropriately decompose this matrix H into our two matrices that we want to have?

27:12.000 --> 27:17.000
So the structure tells me K is a triangular matrix.

27:17.000 --> 27:20.000
This is this 3x3 triangular matrix.

27:20.000 --> 27:23.000
And R is a 3x3 rotation matrix.

27:23.000 --> 27:27.000
So you need to ask yourself, is there actually a matrix decomposition technique that you know,

27:27.000 --> 27:32.000
which decomposes a matrix into a rotation matrix and into a triangular one?

27:32.000 --> 27:34.000
And actually there is one.

27:34.000 --> 27:37.000
So QR decomposition is a technique to do this.

27:37.000 --> 27:45.000
It decomposes a matrix in a matrix Q and a matrix R, where Q is a rotation matrix and R is a triangular matrix.

27:46.000 --> 27:57.000
And we actually don't perform that on H itself because the rotation matrix and the calibration matrix actually swapped.

27:57.000 --> 28:01.000
If they would be sitting the other way around, then the QR decomposition would work.

28:01.000 --> 28:03.000
But now they have the wrong order.

28:03.000 --> 28:10.000
So what we need to do is we simply invert H because then through the inversion, those two matrices swap around.

28:10.000 --> 28:12.000
We do everything on the inverse.

28:12.000 --> 28:22.000
So we perform the operation, the QR decomposition on H inverse, which then gives us a Q and a R.

28:22.000 --> 28:32.000
So applying the QR decomposition here on H inverse will give me Q and R, where Q is the rotation matrix transposed and R is the calibration matrix inverted.

28:32.000 --> 28:39.000
So remember, if you invert a matrix, it doesn't change the upper or lower triangular property.

28:39.000 --> 28:42.000
So we can do this also with K inverse.

28:42.000 --> 28:52.000
So by decomposing this matrix H inverse into QR, I get the rotation matrix and the calibration matrix actually separated from each other.

28:53.000 --> 29:07.000
One thing we need to take care of, our matrix H with K times R is homogeneous matrix and SUS is the calibration matrix homogeneous matrix because R is the standard rotation matrix.

29:07.000 --> 29:11.000
So as a result of this, we need to make sure we have appropriate normalization.

29:11.000 --> 29:23.000
So we can achieve this normalization by dividing the calibration matrix through its element 3, 3 so that we have a 1 over there and the rest of the parameters are appropriately normalized.

29:23.000 --> 29:34.000
So K can be updated as 1 divided by K 3, 3, so the element 3, 3 of the original matrix so that this element becomes 1 and we have our normalized matrix.

29:34.000 --> 29:37.000
And then we have our matrix K.

29:38.000 --> 29:42.000
Okay, one thing we need to take care of as well.

29:42.000 --> 29:50.000
With this, if you compute the decomposition of H inverse, it will lead to a matrix K which has positive diagonal elements.

29:50.000 --> 29:53.000
So all the three elements on the main diagonal are positive.

29:53.000 --> 29:59.000
Consider that the first two elements, so K11 and K22 was our camera constant.

29:59.000 --> 30:04.000
But we defined our coordinate system in a way that the camera constant is negative.

30:04.000 --> 30:10.000
That is that I have the projection center, the image plane in front of it and the point there in the world.

30:10.000 --> 30:13.000
If it would sit on the other side, everything would be fine.

30:13.000 --> 30:16.000
But we defined our coordinate system in that way that it sits here in front.

30:16.000 --> 30:23.000
So what we need to do is we need to perform a rotation of our coordinate system so this is appropriately taken into account.

30:23.000 --> 30:28.000
And we can do this by a rotation of pi around the Z axis.

30:28.000 --> 30:38.000
Because in this way I have a matrix, the rotation matrix turns into minus 1, minus 1, 1 on the main diagonal which exactly flips the two signs of my camera constant.

30:38.000 --> 30:49.000
So what I need to do is I need to, after I have computed in the step before R and K, I need to rotate both of them with a 180 degree rotation.

30:50.000 --> 30:54.000
So the decomposition of H in this QR decomposition still holds.

30:54.000 --> 31:01.000
But I need to make sure I do this to have the coordinate system appropriately defined as we did it in the course.

31:02.000 --> 31:06.000
So to sum up, DLT in a nutshell with three simple steps.

31:07.000 --> 31:13.000
The first thing we need to do, we need to build our matrix M for getting our linear system Mp equals 0.

31:13.000 --> 31:18.000
P are our unknowns and this is the vectorized form of my projection matrix.

31:18.000 --> 31:27.000
And this is straightforward, we just need to put for every observation that we get, we generate those two rows and add them to this matrix M.

31:27.000 --> 31:35.000
So for every point we get those two equations which are two rows in my matrix M and I build up my matrix M.

31:35.000 --> 31:42.000
At least 12 by 12 matrix but can also have more observations by at least 12 I do need.

31:43.000 --> 31:46.000
And then I need to find the null space of that matrix.

31:46.000 --> 31:53.000
And the null space of the matrix I find through singular value decomposition so I run SVD on M which gives me these different matrices.

31:53.000 --> 32:07.000
And I'm only interested here in this matrix V because the last column of this matrix V actually gives me either the null space or the best approximation of the null space.

32:07.000 --> 32:11.000
So the singular vector corresponding to the smallest singular value.

32:11.000 --> 32:20.000
And this is our 12 values and I just rearrange those 12 values again in form of my matrix P and then I get P.

32:20.000 --> 32:24.000
And if I'm happy with P, I'm already done at that point.

32:24.000 --> 32:34.000
If I want to get the individual parameters out of this, so K, X0 and my rotation matrix, I need to do this additional step that we just did.

32:34.000 --> 32:40.000
So we define this matrix H and the vector H and then I can compute the projection center easily.

32:40.000 --> 32:53.000
I need to inverse H, perform the QR decomposition which gives me R and K and I still need to do the rotation and the normalization of K in order to have my proper calibration matrix.

32:53.000 --> 33:04.000
And then I can read out the values, for example, the intrinsics directly out of the calibration matrix because I know in which elements what is written such as principal point or the camera constant.

33:05.000 --> 33:08.000
And that's basically what DLT is about.

33:08.000 --> 33:18.000
So the important thing to notice, we are actually free to map between P and our three informations calibration matrix, rotation matrix and translation vector.

33:18.000 --> 33:20.000
I can map forth and back.

33:20.000 --> 33:22.000
Of course, from here I can go trivial to P.

33:22.000 --> 33:27.000
If I need to break up P, it's slightly more complicated, but we have discussed how that actually works.

33:27.000 --> 33:32.000
We are free to choose the sign of C of my camera constant.

33:32.000 --> 33:43.000
And this basically tells me how I choose my coordinate system and I make sure that the decomposition that I do is actually in line with my coordinate system.

33:43.000 --> 33:47.000
And we showed how we do this for the coordinate system we are here using.

33:47.000 --> 33:55.000
It's very important to note that the solution gets instable when my control points lie on a plane or approximatively on a plane.

33:55.000 --> 34:01.000
And this again was one of those critical surfaces which lead to a rank deficiency in M and I need to make sure that this is not the case.

34:01.000 --> 34:06.000
So I need to choose my control points appropriately that they don't lie on a plane.

34:06.000 --> 34:12.000
And what should also be remarked is that the solution that we provided here is statistically not an optimal solution.

34:12.000 --> 34:15.000
It's a direct solution, so I don't need an initial guess.

34:15.000 --> 34:20.000
But what the solution cannot do is it can, for example, not take into account uncertainties.

34:20.000 --> 34:29.000
Let's say uncertainty about the estimates of my control points or measurement noise that I have in determining the pixel coordinates of those control points.

34:29.000 --> 34:36.000
This information that the approach cannot exploit and thus it cannot be a statistically optimal solution.

34:36.000 --> 34:43.000
Nevertheless, it's a direct solution which is very attractive and typically the starting point for all other operations.

34:43.000 --> 34:53.000
So in sum, the DLT or the direct linear transform is a technique to estimate the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of a camera taking the fine camera model into account.

34:53.000 --> 34:59.000
That means determining these 11 degrees of freedom, six extrinsic, five intrinsic parameters.

34:59.000 --> 35:05.000
And in this way we can compute all the parameters that is needed for the mapping of the uncalibrated camera.

35:05.000 --> 35:12.000
So we get all the parameters to describe how a point from the 3D world is actually mapped into our image plane.

35:12.000 --> 35:19.000
What we need to do for this, we need to have our camera and we need to know at least six 3D coordinates of control points in the environment.

35:19.000 --> 35:22.000
This is kind of a prerequisite for applying this.

35:22.000 --> 35:25.000
But if we have that, we can compute a direct solution.

35:25.000 --> 35:29.000
We don't need an initial guess and we get our parameters out of that system.

35:29.000 --> 35:35.000
And as a result of this, we can calibrate our camera with knowing six points in the environment.

35:35.000 --> 35:40.000
At least, of course, the more points we get, the better our results get.

35:40.000 --> 35:45.000
But that should be clear because we have more information from the observations that we can actually exploit.

35:45.000 --> 35:51.000
So thank you very much for your attention and to this first step towards calibrating a camera.

35:51.000 --> 35:52.000
Thank you.

